armer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: knowledge (a general farming question, not about this farm's current state).\n- Answer it from AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE. In Observation, state what the text says that answers the question; farm readings are optional context.\n- Refuse only when the text does not contain the answer: then say \"The reference text does not cover this.\" and name exactly what is missing. Do not refuse because farm readings are missing — this question does not need them.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (irrigation)\nJudge irrigation timing from the soil-moisture level and its trend and the crop stage. Say whether to irrigate now, wait, or hold, and why.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\nToday: 05 October 2026\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 50394 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information:\n- General guidance: irrigation water depth of 7–8 cm per application is recommended, with frequency varying by crop phase — approximately once every 7 days during germination (1–35 days after planting, DAP), once every 10 days during tillering (36–100 DAP), once every 7 days during grand growth (101–270 DAP), and once every 15 days during maturity (271 DAP to harvest), adjusted for rainfall.\n- Subtropical regions typically need 6–8 irrigations across the season; tropical regions (including Karnataka) may need 20–36 irrigations due to climatic conditions, annual rainfall, planting season, crop duration, and soil type.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Key Facts:\n- Total seasonal water requirement: 1,500–2,500 mm, depending on climate.\n- Karnataka irrigation requirement: 32 irrigations/season; 266 kg water per kg sugar produced.\n- Skip-furrow irrigation: 30–40% water saving.\n- Sprinkler irrigation: 50–60% water economy; drip irrigation: up to 95% application uniformity.\n- Recommended irrigation depth per application: 7–8 cm.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information:\n- **Karnataka-specific data**: 32 irrigations required per season; average yield 85.5 t/ha; irrigation water required to produce 1.0 kg of sugar is 266 kg — among the highest of all sugarcane states reported, reflecting Karnataka's tropical multi-irrigation cropping pattern.\n- **Net irrigation water requirement by technique (Karnataka, mm/year)**: furrow irrigation 1,878 mm; skip furrow 1,408 mm; sprinkler 1,325 mm; drip 1,186 mm — showing drip irrigation can reduce net water requirement by roughly 37% compared to conventional furrow irrigation for the same crop water demand.\n\n### Water-economizing methods\n1. **Skip-furrow/alternate furrow irrigation**: one row is skipped and irrigation applied only to alternate furrows, saving 30–40% water without reducing cane productivity.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Important Recommendations:\n- Given Karnataka's high per-season irrigation requirement (32 irrigations, 266 kg water per kg sugar produced), farmers in the state should prioritize migration from furrow irrigation to drip irrigation, which the source data shows can lower net water requirement from ~1,878 mm/year to ~1,186 mm/year.\n- During monsoon disruptions, adopt the contingency schedule appropriate to the delay: for a 2-week delayed monsoon onset, continue pre-monsoon irrigation intervals (8–10 days) with alternate-furrow application and trash mulching in ratoon; for longer delays (6–8 weeks), shift to a 10-day protective irrigation interval and watch for iron deficiency, which becomes likely under prolonged dry spells (foliar spray of 1% FeSO₄ + 0.5% ZnSO₄ + 2% urea is recommended in that case).\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: How deep should each irrigation be for sugarcane?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Irrigation water depth generally ranges from 7–8 centimeters but varies based on specific field condition as detailed above.\nFarmer: How deep should each irrigation be for sugarcane?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Irrigation water depth generally ranges from 7–8 centimeters but varies based on specific field condition as detailed above. (Full fact-checking was unavailable for this answer; only the local number and name checks ran.)\nFarmer: How deep should each irrigation be for sugarcane?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Irrigation water depth generally ranges from 7–8 centimeters but varies based on specific field condition as detailed above.\n\n# USER QUESTION\nHow deep should each irrigation be?", "farmer": "How deep should each irrigation be?"}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "How deep should each irrigation be?"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8828}, "retrieved": [{"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information", "score": 0.8828, "via": "original"}, {"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Key Facts", "score": 0.855, "via": "original"}, {"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information", "score": 0.851, "via": "original"}, {"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8475, "via": "original"}], "raw_answer": "**Observation**  \n- The reference text states: “irrigation water depth of 7–8 cm per application is recommended.”  \n- It also repeats: “Recommended irrigation depth per application: 7–8 cm.”\n\n**Analysis**  \nThese statements indicate that, for sugarcane in Karnataka, each irrigation event should deliver water to a depth of roughly 7 to 8 centimetres in the soil profile.\n\n**Confidence**  \nHigh – the depth is explicitly given in the agricultural knowledge source.\n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n- **Immediate actions**  \n  - Apply irrigation water so that the wetting front reaches **7–8 cm** depth for each scheduled irrigation.\n\n- **Monitoring advice**  \n  - After irrigation, check the soil‑moisture sensor (or manually probe) to confirm the moisture front has penetrated to the target depth.  \n  - Observe the crop for signs of water stress (wilting, slowed growth) and adjust future applications if the soil dries faster than expected.\n\n- **When to seek expert help**  \n  - If soil moisture does not reach the 7–8 cm depth despite applying the recommended water volume.  \n  - If you consistently observe water‑stress symptoms even after irrigating to the suggested depth, indicating possible issues with soil texture, drainage, or irrigation uniformity.", "claims": ["supported: Irrigation water depth of 7–8 cm per application is recommended", "supported: Recommended irrigation depth per application: 7–8 cm", "supported: Each irrigation event should deliver water to a depth of roughly 7 to 8 cm in the soil profile", "supported: Apply irrigation water so that the wetting front reaches 7–8 cm depth for each scheduled irrigation", "unsupported: After irrigation, check the soil‑moisture sensor to confirm the moisture front has penetrated to the target depth", "unsupported: Observe the crop for signs of water stress (wilting, slowed growth) and adjust future applications if the soil dries faster than expected", "unsupported: If soil moisture does not reach the 7–8 cm depth despite applying the recommended water volume", "unsupported: If you consistently observe water‑stress symptoms even after irrigating to the suggested depth, indicating possible issues with soil texture, drainage, or irrigation uniformity"], "unsupported": ["unsupported: After irrigation, check the soil‑moisture sensor to confirm the moisture front has penetrated to the target depth", "unsupported: Observe the crop for signs of water stress (wilting, slowed growth) and adjust future applications if the soil dries faster than expected", "unsupported: If soil moisture does not reach the 7–8 cm depth despite applying the recommended water volume", "unsupported: If you consistently observe water‑stress symptoms even after irrigating to the suggested depth, indicating possible issues with soil texture, drainage, or irrigation uniformity"], "final_answer": "Please double-check before acting: \"After irrigation, check the soil‑moisture sensor to confirm the moisture front has penetrated to the target depth\" (not in Gage's sources); \"Observe the crop for signs of water stress (wilting, slowed growth) and adjust future applications if the soil dries faster than expected\" (not in Gage's sources); \"If soil moisture does not reach the 7–8 cm depth despite applying the recommended water volume\" (not in Gage's sources); +1.\n\n**Observation**  \n- The reference text states: “irrigation water depth of 7–8 cm per application is recommended.”  \n- It also repeats: “Recommended irrigation depth per application: 7–8 cm.”\n\n**Analysis**  \nThese statements indicate that, for sugarcane in Karnataka, each irrigation event should deliver water to a depth of roughly 7 to 8 centimetres in the soil profile.\n\n**Confidence**  \nHigh – the depth is explicitly given in the agricultural knowledge source.\n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n- **Immediate actions**  \n  - Apply irrigation water so that the wetting front reaches **7–8 cm** depth for each scheduled irrigation.\n\n- **Monitoring advice**  \n  - After irrigation, check the soil‑moisture sensor (or manually probe) to confirm the moisture front has penetrated to the target depth.  \n  - Observe the crop for signs of water stress (wilting, slowed growth) and adjust future applications if the soil dries faster than expected.\n\n- **When to seek expert help**  \n  - If soil moisture does not reach the 7–8 cm depth despite applying the recommended water volume.  \n  - If you consistently observe water‑stress symptoms even after irrigating to the suggested depth, indicating possible issues with soil texture, drainage, or irrigation uniformity.", "fact_check": "checked", "fact_check_seconds": 1.27, "groq_calls": [{"purpose": "answer", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "prompt_tokens": 1679, "completion_tokens": 511, "seconds": 3.22, "status": "ok", "seq": 1}, {"purpose": "fact_check", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 983, "completion_tokens": 578, "seconds": 1.27, "status": "ok", "seq": 2}]}
